s own. Calling it preservation, however, should not settle the question of what evidence it preserves. The language of rescue can allow a developer to present a choice among public goods as though no choice had occurred.\n\nConversely, an insistence on keeping every worn stair or obsolete pipe can mistake accumulated matter for accumulated knowledge. Some replacements make a building usable enough to remain part of public life. Maintenance is not an interruption of history; it is one of the activities history consists of. Yet this observation grants no blanket permission to modernize. The relevant question is whether a particular alteration sustains the building''s intelligibility or suppresses it, and to whom. An engineer, a former worker, and a hurried commuter may read different evidence in the same space.\n\nA preservation report ought, then, to describe its losses as candidly as its achievements. It should distinguish what is documented, what is inferred, and what has been deliberately newly made. Such a report would give up the comforting claim that the building has simply been returned to itself. In exchange, it would offer something more durable: a record that later users can dispute. The honest building need not announce every intervention on its walls. The honest institution must nevertheless leave the argument open, rather than hide its own decisions behind the supposed command of the past.","passage_id":"cars-repair-oct-p07","passage_title":"Architecture: preservation and the authority of repair","passage_order":4,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-repair-oct-q44', '{"id":"cars-repair-oct-q44","topic":"Challenge to an argument","section":"CARS","skill":"Reasoning beyond the text","difficulty":"Hard (AI-estimated)","prompt":"Which finding would most directly weaken the author''s criticism of returning the station to its architectural drawings?","options":["The committee finds that reproducing the drawings is less expensive than preserving the metal roof and proposes directing the savings to public access.","Period passenger campaigns treated completing the tile roof as central to the station''s public identity and described the metal roof as provisional.","The committee includes transportation historians who have previously restored stations and have published studies documenting the importance of architectural drawings.","Present-day passengers recognize the metal roof as a familiar local landmark, and early photographs show it during the station''s busiest working years."],"answer":1,"explanation":"B is correct. The opening challenges the authority of an unrealized designer''s intention over a building''s actual social life. Period passenger campaigns making the planned roof part of the station''s public identity would give that plan a historical basis beyond the architect''s paperwork. This weakens the example''s contrast between planned form and lived history without making restoration obligatory.\n\nA: A practical benefit can justify a project, but it does not supply historical evidence that weakens the criticism of drawing-based authenticity.\n\nC: General expertise and past work do not supply evidence that this planned roof was important to the particular station''s lived history.\n\nD: This connects the metal roof to actual use and supports the opening criticism rather than weakening it.\n\nConcept: To weaken a specific argument, alter a premise connecting its evidence to its conclusion, not merely the popularity or authority of the decision makers.","hints":["Identify the contrast between the designer''s plan and the station''s social history.","Which finding makes the planned roof part of that social history?"],"target_seconds":105,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored architectural criticism for this pilot. Fictional examples; no external essay, quotation, or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"The old station acquired its celebrated roof almost by accident. The architect''s drawing specified tiles; a shortage supplied corrugated metal instead. When a preservation committee proposed replacing the metal with tiles, it called the project a return to authenticity. The roof that had actually sheltered generations of passengers was thus condemned for failing to resemble a roof that had never existed. The episode is amusing only until one notices how often a building''s surviving life must defend itself against its paperwork.\n\nArchitectural drawings deserve attention. They record ambitions that an unfinished or altered building may obscure. But intention is not the sole source of historical authority. A station is also a succession of bargains: between designer and builder, weather and material, occupants and the schedules that bring them together. To freeze one moment as the original is to select from that succession, not to escape selection. A committee that chooses the architect''s drawing has made an interpretation just as surely as one that chooses the familiar metal roof.\n\nThe most persuasive defense of preservation is therefore less theatrical than a recovery of origins. Old buildings make earlier decisions available for inspection. Their unevenness can expose how a city accommodated uses that no planner anticipated. This evidentiary value is easily destroyed, but it is not secured merely by keeping a facade. A perfectly retained street elevation may conceal an interior from which every trace of working life has been erased. What survives for a photograph need not survive for inquiry.\n\nThat distinction should trouble admirers of adaptive reuse as well as its opponents. A warehouse converted into apartments can retain the name of its former industry while removing the loading doors, circulation routes, and divisions that made the industry intelligible. The conversion may be worthwhile; new housing has claims of its own. Calling it preservation, however, should not settle the question of what evidence it preserves. The language of rescue can allow a developer to present a choice among public goods as though no choice had occurred.\n\nConversely, an insistence on keeping every worn stair or obsolete pipe can mistake accumulated matter for accumulated knowledge. Some replacements make a building usable enough to remain part of public life. Maintenance is not an interruption of history; it is one of the activities history consists of. Yet this observation grants no blanket permission to modernize. The relevant question is whether a particular alteration sustains the building''s intelligibility or suppresses it, and to whom. An engineer, a former worker, and a hurried commuter may read different evidence in the same space.\n\nA preservation report ought, then, to describe its losses as candidly as its achievements. It should distinguish what is documented, what is inferred, and what has been deliberately newly made. Such a report would give up the comforting claim that the building has simply been returned to itself. In exchange, it would offer something more durable: a record that later users can dispute. The honest building need not announce every intervention on its walls. The honest institution must nevertheless leave the argument open, rather than hide its own decisions behind the supposed command of the past.","passage_id":"cars-repair-oct-p07","passage_title":"Architecture: preservation and the authority of repair","passage_order":5,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-measures-oct-q45', '{"id":"cars-measures-oct-q45","topic":"Central argument","section":"CARS","skill":"Foundations of comprehension","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"Which statement best captures the author''s proposed approach to evaluating institutions?","options":["Use narrative accounts to determine which institutions should be exempt from common measures.","Treat comparable counts as the primary verdict, with purpose-based reviews explaining exceptions.","Reward improvements in a common measure while expanding that measure to include omitted activities.","Cross-check routine counts with assessments that examine purposes through a different evidentiary route."],"answer":3,"explanation":"D is correct. The author accepts counting but shows how categories omit services and rewarded measures change behavior. The remedy is a second view based on actual encounters and institutional purposes, whose errors need not follow the first measure''s incentives. Counts become stronger when corroborated, while divergence warrants investigation rather than an automatic verdict.\n\nA: The passage rejects refusing comparison as a shelter from scrutiny; narratives are not an exemption device.\n\nB: The metaphor denies the spreadsheet governing authority, and the final paragraph treats disagreement as information rather than explaining away exceptions.\n\nC: Expanding a metric can still expose it to the incentive problem. The proposed remedy separates routine records from high-stakes rewards and adds another view.\n\nConcept: Identify the author''s proposed use of a tool, rather than reducing the argument to support for or opposition to the tool itself.","hints":["Connect the incentive problem to the final paragraph''s proposed remedy.","What makes the second assessment a different view rather than an expanded version of the same target?"],"target_seconds":95,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored essay on measurement and public institutions. Fictional library example; no external essay or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a spreadsheet. Its columns listed visits, loans, and cost per transaction; branches arranged themselves into an apparently natural ranking. Nobody had to say that borrowing a novel was the same kind of achievement as using a quiet table to fill out a housing application. The table had performed that assimilation already, counting the former and leaving the latter difficult to see. Numbers had not ended the argument about a library''s purpose. They had advanced one side of it without acquiring a speaker''s name.\n\nThis is not an objection to counting. A librarian who reports an increase in evening attendance may reveal a need that anecdotes missed. A common measure can also restrain favoritism: it is harder to reward a politically connected branch for its vague reputation when another can demonstrate extensive use. There is real democratic value in asking institutions to show what they do. The trouble begins when showing becomes synonymous with whatever a reporting system happens to record.\n\nMeasures are often defended as imperfect but better than nothing. The defense conceals a choice. A figure may be compared with a different figure, with a narrative account, or with a combination of observations. \"Nothing\" is frequently the name given to evidence that an administrative system has not learned to process. The person explaining that a library is the neighborhood''s only heated public room has supplied information, even if no column awaits it. Converting that fact into a number could make it travel farther through the budget process; it could also detach it from the circumstances that explain its importance.\n\nThe demand that every benefit receive a common unit promises comparability at a price. It can make unlike services appear interchangeable and turn a decision about purposes into a calculation about efficiency. Yet refusing comparison altogether has its own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":0,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-measures-oct-q46', '{"id":"cars-measures-oct-q46","topic":"Tone and personification","section":"CARS","skill":"Foundations of comprehension","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"Calling the spreadsheet the meeting''s \"most confident speaker\" primarily conveys:","options":["ironic concern that an impersonal ranking can speak with unearned authority","hostility toward librarians who describe their services publicly","nostalgia for meetings held before numerical records were available","admiration for the spreadsheet''s capacity to express every participant''s priorities"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A is correct. The personification makes an apparently neutral spreadsheet into an advocate. The opening then shows how its categories favor one understanding of achievement without openly naming that position. The tone is ironic: confidence and apparent naturalness conceal choices rather than certify that the ranking represents everyone''s purposes.\n\nB: The target is the authority of the reporting system, not public description of library services.\n\nC: No earlier era of meetings is invoked; the objection concerns how a current measure frames judgment.\n\nD: The spreadsheet omits relevant activities and therefore does not express all priorities.\n\nConcept: Determine tone from what a rhetorical device exposes in context, not from whether its literal wording sounds complimentary.","hints":["Ask whether the spreadsheet''s confidence is endorsed or questioned.","Connect the personification to the unnamed side of the argument."],"target_seconds":85,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored essay on measurement and public institutions. Fictional library example; no external essay or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a spreadsheet. Its columns listed visits, loans, and cost per transaction; branches arranged themselves into an apparently natural ranking. Nobody had to say that borrowing a novel was the same kind of achievement as using a quiet table to fill out a housing application. The table had performed that assimilation already, counting the former and leaving the latter difficult to see. Numbers had not ended the argument about a library''s purpose. They had advanced one side of it without acquiring a speaker''s name.\n\nThis is not an objection to counting. A librarian who reports an increase in evening attendance may reveal a need that anecdotes missed. A common measure can also restrain favoritism: it is harder to reward a politically connected branch for its vague reputation when another can demonstrate extensive use. There is real democratic value in asking institutions to show what they do. The trouble begins when showing becomes synonymous with whatever a reporting system happens to record.\n\nMeasures are often defended as imperfect but better than nothing. The defense conceals a choice. A figure may be compared with a different figure, with a narrative account, or with a combination of observations. \"Nothing\" is frequently the name given to evidence that an administrative system has not learned to process. The person explaining that a library is the neighborhood''s only heated public room has supplied information, even if no column awaits it. Converting that fact into a number could make it travel farther through the budget process; it could also detach it from the circumstances that explain its importance.\n\nThe demand that every benefit receive a common unit promises comparability at a price. It can make unlike services appear interchangeable and turn a decision about purposes into a calculation about efficiency. Yet refusing comparison altogether has its own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":1,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-measures-oct-q47', '{"id":"cars-measures-oct-q47","topic":"Function of a concession","section":"CARS","skill":"Reasoning within the text","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"Why does the author discuss politically connected branches in the second paragraph?","options":["To treat a branch''s higher transaction count as sufficient evidence that its funding reflects a fair public judgment","To suggest that political connections, rather than service demand, explain differences in branches'' reported use","To show a circumstance in which a shared measure can improve accountability","To explain why narratives are more reliable than transaction counts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"C is correct. The example acknowledges that comparable evidence of use can constrain favoritism based on a branch''s vague reputation or political connections. It gives measurement a genuine democratic benefit, strengthening the later distinction between using metrics and allowing them to define an institution''s whole purpose.\n\nA: A shared measure can restrain favoritism, but the author does not treat a higher count as sufficient evidence of fair funding.\n\nB: The example concerns political advantage based on vague reputation. It does not explain numerical use differences as products of political connections.\n\nD: In this example, a shared numerical measure is useful; it does not establish narrative superiority.\n\nConcept: A concession can establish the useful scope of a practice before the author limits a stronger claim about it.","hints":["Notice the positive description of democratic value.","What problem does evidence of extensive use help address?"],"target_seconds":85,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored essay on measurement and public institutions. Fictional library example; no external essay or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a