 rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":2,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-measures-oct-q48', '{"id":"cars-measures-oct-q48","topic":"Interpretation of a metaphor","section":"CARS","skill":"Reasoning within the text","difficulty":"Hard (AI-estimated)","prompt":"The instruction that a spreadsheet be \"an exhibit\" rather than \"its constitution\" means that the spreadsheet should:","options":["serve as evidence in deliberation rather than determine the purposes governing it","contain narrative annotations instead of quantitative entries","be displayed publicly rather than stored in the institution''s private records","record legal obligations rather than compare the services actually delivered"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A is correct. An exhibit contributes evidence to an argument; a constitution establishes the governing framework. The preceding sentences concern comparison among purposes and what comparisons omit. The metaphor therefore assigns numbers a role within deliberation while rejecting their authority to set all its ends and rules.\n\nB: The author permits quantitative entries and asks that their role and omissions be examined.\n\nC: Physical display versus storage is not the contrast developed by the paragraph.\n\nD: The metaphor does not refer to constitutional law or propose replacing service measures with legal records.\n\nConcept: Use the argument surrounding a metaphor to identify the relationship it expresses, rather than applying each word literally.","hints":["Compare the role of evidence with the role of governing principles.","Read the sentence in light of the preceding discussion of purposes."],"target_seconds":95,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored essay on measurement and public institutions. Fictional library example; no external essay or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a spreadsheet. Its columns listed visits, loans, and cost per transaction; branches arranged themselves into an apparently natural ranking. Nobody had to say that borrowing a novel was the same kind of achievement as using a quiet table to fill out a housing application. The table had performed that assimilation already, counting the former and leaving the latter difficult to see. Numbers had not ended the argument about a library''s purpose. They had advanced one side of it without acquiring a speaker''s name.\n\nThis is not an objection to counting. A librarian who reports an increase in evening attendance may reveal a need that anecdotes missed. A common measure can also restrain favoritism: it is harder to reward a politically connected branch for its vague reputation when another can demonstrate extensive use. There is real democratic value in asking institutions to show what they do. The trouble begins when showing becomes synonymous with whatever a reporting system happens to record.\n\nMeasures are often defended as imperfect but better than nothing. The defense conceals a choice. A figure may be compared with a different figure, with a narrative account, or with a combination of observations. \"Nothing\" is frequently the name given to evidence that an administrative system has not learned to process. The person explaining that a library is the neighborhood''s only heated public room has supplied information, even if no column awaits it. Converting that fact into a number could make it travel farther through the budget process; it could also detach it from the circumstances that explain its importance.\n\nThe demand that every benefit receive a common unit promises comparability at a price. It can make unlike services appear interchangeable and turn a decision about purposes into a calculation about efficiency. Yet refusing comparison altogether has its own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":3,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-measures-oct-q49', '{"id":"cars-measures-oct-q49","topic":"Application to incentives","section":"CARS","skill":"Reasoning beyond the text","difficulty":"Hard (AI-estimated)","prompt":"A school rewarded for graduation rates makes courses easier and increases graduation without improving students'' demonstrated skills. This finding most directly supports which passage claim?","options":["Selecting a common unit makes comparisons reflect the institutions'' shared purposes.","Repeated measurement reduces the importance of omitted activities in a comparison.","A reported improvement can be real while its relation to the institution''s purpose changes.","A second evaluation should place greater weight on the same outcome used to distribute rewards."],"answer":2,"explanation":"C is correct. More students really graduate, just as more books may circulate after libraries respond to loan targets. The concern is that this real improvement can reflect behavior adapted to the reward rather than improvement in the broader purpose, here learning. The finding supports changed interpretation of a measure, not the claim that every favorable number is fabricated.\n\nA: The school example shows that a shared graduation count need not capture the purpose of education adequately.\n\nB: Repeating the same measurement does not recover skills or activities omitted from it.\n\nD: The final paragraph proposes a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives of the first, not greater dependence on the same rewarded outcome.\n\nConcept: A beyond-the-text application should preserve the causal relationship in the passage: rewarded measure, behavioral adaptation, and changed meaning of improvement.","hints":["Identify what the school is rewarded for and what it changes.","Compare that chain with the borrowing example in the fifth paragraph."],"target_seconds":100,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored essay on measurement and public institutions. Fictional library example; no external essay or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a spreadsheet. Its columns listed visits, loans, and cost per transaction; branches arranged themselves into an apparently natural ranking. Nobody had to say that borrowing a novel was the same kind of achievement as using a quiet table to fill out a housing application. The table had performed that assimilation already, counting the former and leaving the latter difficult to see. Numbers had not ended the argument about a library''s purpose. They had advanced one side of it without acquiring a speaker''s name.\n\nThis is not an objection to counting. A librarian who reports an increase in evening attendance may reveal a need that anecdotes missed. A common measure can also restrain favoritism: it is harder to reward a politically connected branch for its vague reputation when another can demonstrate extensive use. There is real democratic value in asking institutions to show what they do. The trouble begins when showing becomes synonymous with whatever a reporting system happens to record.\n\nMeasures are often defended as imperfect but better than nothing. The defense conceals a choice. A figure may be compared with a different figure, with a narrative account, or with a combination of observations. \"Nothing\" is frequently the name given to evidence that an administrative system has not learned to process. The person explaining that a library is the neighborhood''s only heated public room has supplied information, even if no column awaits it. Converting that fact into a number could make it travel farther through the budget process; it could also detach it from the circumstances that explain its importance.\n\nThe demand that every benefit receive a common unit promises comparability at a price. It can make unlike services appear interchangeable and turn a decision about purposes into a calculation about efficiency. Yet refusing comparison altogether has its own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":4,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cars-measures-oct-q50', '{"id":"cars-measures-oct-q50","topic":"Inference from corroborating evidence","section":"CARS","skill":"Reasoning beyond the text","difficulty":"Hard (AI-estimated)","prompt":"A library has few loans but claims to provide substantial assistance with housing applications. A sample of visitor encounters confirms that assistance. According to the author''s final proposal, what should evaluators infer?","options":["The discrepancy identifies a service the loan count does not capture.","The sampled encounters establish that the library uses its entire budget efficiently.","The original loan count is inaccurate and should be corrected upward.","The library''s own account should replace comparisons with other branches."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A is correct. The second view confirms a consequential activity outside the transaction count. The low loan number can remain accurate while failing to capture that service. The proposed cross-check treats divergence as information about what each measure represents, not proof that a count is false, the institution is wholly efficient, or comparison should cease.\n\nB: Evidence of a valuable service does not establish efficient use of every part of a budget.\n\nC: Assistance with applications does not make the reported number of loans incorrect.\n\nD: The author supports comparison and scrutiny; a corroborated account adds evidence rather than replacing all comparative evaluation.\n\nConcept: An accurate count can omit a valuable activity. Independent evidence of that activity changes interpretation of the count, not necessarily its numerical accuracy.","hints":["Can the loan count be accurate while omitting a valuable service?","Apply the final paragraph''s treatment of disagreement between two views."],"target_seconds":95,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored essay on measurement and public institutions. Fictional library example; no external essay or copyrighted test passage adapted. All questions rely on the supplied passage.","passage":"At a meeting about library funding, the most confident speaker was a spreadsheet. Its columns listed visits, loans, and cost per transaction; branches arranged themselves into an apparently natural ranking. Nobody had to say that borrowing a novel was the same kind of achievement as using a quiet table to fill out a housing application. The table had performed that assimilation already, counting the former and leaving the latter difficult to see. Numbers had not ended the argument about a library''s purpose. They had advanced one side of it without acquiring a speaker''s name.\n\nThis is not an objection to counting. A librarian who reports an increase in evening attendance may reveal a need that anecdotes missed. A common measure can also restrain favoritism: it is harder to reward a politically connected branch for its vague reputation when another can demonstrate extensive use. There is real democratic value in asking institutions to show what they do. The trouble begins when showing becomes synonymous with whatever a reporting system happens to record.\n\nMeasures are often defended as imperfect but better than nothing. The defense conceals a choice. A figure may be compared with a different figure, with a narrative account, or with a combination of observations. \"Nothing\" is frequently the name given to evidence that an administrative system has not learned to process. The person explaining that a library is the neighborhood''s only heated public room has supplied information, even if no column awaits it. Converting that fact into a number could make it travel farther through the budget process; it could also detach it from the circumstances that explain its importance.\n\nThe demand that every benefit receive a common unit promises comparability at a price. It can make unlike services appear interchangeable and turn a decision about purposes into a calculation about efficiency. Yet refusing comparison altogether has its own price. Funds are finite, and a claim that one institution is incomparable may protect it from scrutiny rather than illuminate its value. A careful public argument must therefore compare while preserving an account of what the comparison leaves out. The spreadsheet should be an exhibit in that argument, not its constitution.\n\nThere is a further complication. Once a measure distributes rewards, institutions adapt to it. A branch judged by loans may encourage short, frequent borrowing or avoid services that take time without producing a transaction. The resulting improvement in the reported figure is not necessarily fictitious: more books may indeed circulate. But the figure becomes a record of both the original activity and the institution''s response to being measured. Treating it as an untouched window on performance ignores a change produced by the window itself.\n\nThe practical remedy is to separate routine records from high-stakes rewards. Institutions can still report counts, but funding reviews should periodically examine a sample of actual encounters, using questions chosen to test the institution''s stated purposes rather than merely repeat its target. Such reviews cost time and can be conducted badly. Their purpose is not to find an unmeasured truth; it is to create a second view whose errors need not follow the incentives embedded in the first. If both views agree, the numerical claim gains support. If they diverge, the discrepancy is information, not administrative failure.","passage_id":"cars-measures-oct-p08","passage_title":"Public policy: the work done by measures","passage_order":5,"figures":[]}'::jsonb),
('cp-discrete-oct-q11', '{"id":"cp-discrete-oct-q11","topic":"Electrochemistry and thermodynamics","section":"C/P","skill":"Scientific reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"A galvanic cell has E°cell = +0.30 V for a reaction transferring two electrons. A catalyst is added without changing concentrations or temperature. Which pair correctly describes the standard Gibbs energy and the effect on E°cell?","options":["ΔG° < 0; E°cell decreases","ΔG° = 0; E°cell is unchanged","ΔG° < 0; E°cell is unchanged","ΔG° > 0; E°cell increases"],"answer":2,"explanation":"C is correct. ΔG° = -nFE°cell, so a positive standard cell potential gives a negative standard Gibbs energy. A catalyst changes the rate of reaching equilibrium by lowering activation barriers; it does not change the free-energy difference between reactants and products, the equilibrium constant, or the standard cell potential.\n\nA: A catalyst can accelerate electron transfer without decreasing the standard potential.\n\nB: A favorable reaction need not be at standard-state equilibrium. Zero ΔG° would correspond to E°cell = 0.\n\nD: The sign relation is reversed, and a catalyst does not raise the thermodynamic driving force.\n\nConcept: Kinetics determines how rapidly a reaction proceeds; thermodynamics determines its driving force and equilibrium.","hints":["Use ΔG° = -nFE°cell to determine the sign.","Ask whether a catalyst changes endpoints or the path between them."],"target_seconds":80,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('cp-discrete-oct-q12', '{"id":"cp-discrete-oct-q12","topic":"Organic stereochemistry","section":"C/P","skill":"Scientific reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"An optically pure chiral secondary alkyl halide reacts through an SN1 pathway in an achiral solvent. Which explanation best accounts for reduced enantiomeric excess in the substitution product mixture?","options":["The solvent converts both enantiomers to the same achiral constitutional isomer.","The leaving group and nucleophile exchange positions in a single backside attack.","A planar intermediate permits nucleophilic attack from either face.","The carbon stereocenter becomes permanently sp2-hybridized in the substitution product."],"answer":2,"explanation":"C is correct. SN1 substitution proceeds through a carbocation intermediate with an approximately planar geometry at the reactive carbon. Attack from either face can produce both configurations, reducing enantiomeric excess and therefore optical rotation. Exact racemization need not occur because ion pairing and solvent effects can favor one face.\n\nA: Enantiomers have the same connectivity; the mechanism does not require conversion to a different constitutional isomer.\n\nB: A concerted backside attack describes SN2 and usually produces stereochemical inversion rather than attack from both faces.\n\nD: The substitution product generally restores tetrahedral geometry. Planarity describes the intermediate, not necessarily the final carbon.\n\nConcept: Track stereochemistry through the mechanism; transient planarity can reduce optical purity without removing the final stereocenter.","hints":["Identify the geometry of the SN1 intermediate.","Consider whether both faces are accessible to an incoming nucleophile."],"target_seconds":80,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('cp-discrete-oct-q13', '{"id":"cp-discrete-oct-q13","topic":"Electric circuits and power","section":"C/P","skill":"Quantitative reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"Two resistors, R and 2R, are connected in parallel to an ideal battery. Their total power is P. They are then connected in series to the same battery. What is the new total power?","options":["P/9","2P/9","P/3","3P/2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"B is correct. The parallel equivalent resistance is (1/R + 1/(2R))^-1 = 2R/3. The series equivalent resistance is 3R. At fixed voltage, power is V^2/Req, so Pseries/Pparallel = (2R/3)/(3R) = 2/9. The ideal battery assumption keeps the voltage the same after rewiring.\n\nA: This misses the factor of two in the parallel equivalent resistance.\n\nC: This compares the series resistance with R rather than with the original parallel equivalent.\n\nD: This predicts greater power despite the increase in equivalent resistance at fixed voltage.\n\nConcept: Compute the equivalent resistance before comparing circuit power; a fixed-voltage source makes power inversely proportional to resistance.","hints":["Find each configuration''s equivalent resistance.","Use a ratio so the battery voltage cancels."],"target_seconds":95,"version":1,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('bb-discrete-oct-q24', '{"id":"bb-discrete-oct-q24","topic":"Meiosis and genetic linkage","section":"B/B","skill":"Quantitative reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"An individual has linked alleles arranged AB/ab. The recombination frequency between the loci is 20%. Assuming the two reciprocal recombinant gamete types are equally frequent, what fraction of this individual''s gametes are Ab?","options":["10%","20%","40%","80%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A is correct. The parental haplotypes are AB and ab. Recombinant gametes are Ab and aB. A recombination frequency of 20% describes their combined frequency, so each reciprocal recombinant type accounts for 10% under the stated assumption. Each parental type then accounts for 40%.\n\nB: This assigns the entire recombinant fraction to one of the two reciprocal types.\n\nC: This is the frequency of each parental haplotype, not a recombinant haplotype.\n\nD: This is the combined parental fraction.\n\nConcept: Recombination frequency is the total frequency of recombinant gametes, not the frequency of each recombinant class.","hints":["Identify which haplotypes are parental from the slash notation.","Split the total recombinant fraction between the two specified reciprocal types."],"target_seconds":75,"version":1,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('bb-discrete-oct-q25', '{"id":"bb-discrete-oct-q25","topic":"Protein targeting and secretion","section":"B/B","skill":"Scientific reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"A secreted protein retains a normal amino acid sequence except that its N-terminal endoplasmic-reticulum signal peptide is deleted. No alternative targeting signal is present. Which outcome is most likely?","options":["The protein is imported into mitochondria by its ordinary peptide backbone.","The protein enters the ER but bypasses Golgi processing.","Translation stops because cytosolic ribosomes cannot read its mRNA.","The protein remains in the cytosol rather than entering the conventional secretory pathway."],"answer":3,"explanation":"D is correct. The ER signal peptide directs a nascent secretory protein toward the ER translocation machinery. Without it or an alternative targeting signal, translation can proceed on cytosolic ribosomes, but the protein is not normally delivered into the ER lumen. It therefore does not proceed through the conventional ER-Golgi secretion route. Actual stability in the cytosol may vary.\n\nA: Mitochondrial import requires targeting information; an ordinary backbone is not sufficient.\n\nB: Deletion blocks the targeting step before ER entry, rather than selectively removing Golgi processing.\n\nC: Cytosolic ribosomes initiate translation of secretory-protein mRNAs; the signal peptide changes targeting, not readability of the genetic code.\n\nConcept: Subcellular targeting signals direct localization; losing a targeting signal need not prevent synthesis of the protein itself.","hints":["Distinguish translation from delivery into the ER.","Trace the first step of the conventional secretory route."],"target_seconds":85,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('bb-discrete-oct-q26', '{"id":"bb-discrete-oct-q26","topic":"Endocrine feedback","section":"B/B","skill":"Scientific reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"An antibody activates thyroid TSH receptors independently of circulating TSH. The pituitary and hypothalamus respond normally to thyroid hormone. Which pattern is most likely?","options":["High thyroid hormone, low TSH, low TRH","Low thyroid hormone, low TSH, high TRH","High thyroid hormone, high TSH, low TRH","Low thyroid hormone, high TSH, high TRH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A is correct. Receptor stimulation promotes thyroid hormone production even when pituitary TSH is low. Elevated thyroid hormone feeds back on both the pituitary and hypothalamus, suppressing TSH and TRH, respectively. The antibody bypasses the ordinary need for elevated TSH; it does not remove feedback regulation of upstream hormone release.\n\nB: Activating the thyroid receptor should raise thyroid output; the paired low thyroid hormone and low TSH do not describe the given mechanism.\n\nC: Normal negative feedback suppresses TSH as thyroid hormone rises, so high TSH is inconsistent with the stated intact feedback response.\n\nD: This would fit loss of thyroid output rather than persistent activation of its stimulatory receptor.\n\nConcept: Separate hormone concentration from receptor activation when an external stimulus bypasses an endocrine feedback loop.","hints":["Start with the consequence of activating thyroid TSH receptors.","Then apply thyroid hormone feedback to the pituitary and hypothalamus."],"target_seconds":85,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('ps-discrete-oct-q37', '{"id":"ps-discrete-oct-q37","topic":"Attribution and situational constraints","section":"P/S","skill":"Scientific reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"An observer labels a student lazy after seeing the student arrive late, without considering that the bus route was suspended. This judgment most directly illustrates:","options":["cognitive dissonance caused by the observer''s own tardiness","fundamental attribution error by the observer","the actor-observer difference demonstrated across both people","self-serving bias in the late student''s account"],"answer":1,"explanation":"B is correct. The observer emphasizes a dispositional trait while overlooking a relevant situational constraint. That is the fundamental attribution error. The description provides the observer''s explanation but no explanation from the actor, so it does not demonstrate the full actor-observer contrast. Identifying the amount of evidence given prevents choosing a related but broader label.\n\nA: The observer is not described as late or as experiencing inconsistent beliefs and behavior.\n\nC: This related phenomenon requires contrasting the actor''s and observer''s attributions; only the latter is described.\n\nD: The student''s own explanation is not supplied, so there is no evidence of claiming credit or externalizing blame.\n\nConcept: Choose the bias demonstrated by the available evidence; do not infer an unreported comparison merely because a related term is familiar.","hints":["Whose explanation is actually reported?","Notice the preference for a trait over the stated situational obstacle."],"target_seconds":80,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb),
('ps-discrete-oct-q38', '{"id":"ps-discrete-oct-q38","topic":"Reliability and validity","section":"P/S","skill":"Scientific reasoning","difficulty":"Medium (AI-estimated)","prompt":"A survey intended to measure social isolation gives nearly identical scores on repeated administrations, but its scores mainly track household income and do not track independently measured social contact. Which interpretation is best supported?","options":["High test-retest reliability with questionable construct validity","High construct validity because the repeated scores are stable","Low reliability because income is measured consistently","Low test-retest reliability with strong construct validity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A is correct. Stable scores across repeated administrations support test-retest reliability. They do not establish that the instrument measures social isolation. Its stronger relationship with income and weak relationship with an independent social-contact measure raise a construct-validity concern. Social contact is not a perfect definition of isolation, so the evidence raises a concern rather than proving complete invalidity.\n\nB: Reliability is consistency; validity concerns whether the intended construct is measured. One does not guarantee the other.\n\nC: Tracking a different variable concerns construct interpretation, not necessarily inconsistency across measurements.\n\nD: The stable repeated scores oppose low test-retest reliability, and the described associations do not support strong construct validity.\n\nConcept: A measurement can consistently measure the wrong thing; assess reliability and validity separately.","hints":["Separate score stability from what the scores represent.","Ask which evidence addresses repeated measurement and which addresses the intended construct."],"target_seconds":85,"version":2,"source":"Original independently authored practice question; established foundational science.","passage":null}'::jsonb)
on conflict(id) do nothing;
